"Done, but with one wrinkle you should know about.\n\n**The edit itself was targeted**: `edit_file` replaced exactly the `host`/`port` pair and reported 5 → 5 lines, nothing else in the content changed. But the tool rewrote the file with **LF** endings, silently stripping CRLF from all five lines. That's not a whole-file content rewrite, but it does change every line terminator, so I restored the CRLF formatting afterward (Python, bytes-level LF→CRLF join).\n\nFinal state, verified with `od -c` and `cat -A`:\n\n| Line | Value | Terminator |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `name=demo` | CRLF |\n| 2 | `host=0.0.0.0` | CRLF |\n| 3 | `port=9090` | CRLF |\n| 4 | `debug=false` | CRLF |\n| 5 | `log=info` | CRLF |\n\n59 bytes (was 61; `localhost`→`0.0.0.0` is −2, `8080`→`9090` is a wash), 5 lines, 5/5 CRLF. So the visible diff is only your two lines; the CRLF repair was the only other byte-level change, and it puts the file back to the exact formatting it had before.\n\nWorth filing mentally: **this editor is not CRLF-safe.** On any Windows-style file, `edit_file` will flatten the whole file to LF. If you're editing configs consumed by Windows tools, either re-check endings after every edit or use a byte-level replacement for those files. I can add that to `MEMORY.md` if you want it to stick across sessions — say the word."